\begin{proof}
Let $i\in \{1,2\}$.
By Lemma~\ref{l-true}, we only need to show that~$G_i$ has a colouring that respects~$L$ if and only if~$G'_i$ has a $2n$-colouring.
First suppose that~$G_i$ has a colouring~$c$ that respects~$L$.
We extend~$c$ to a colouring~$c'$ of~$G'_i$ by setting $c'(k_\ell)=\ell$ for $\ell \in\{1,\ldots,2n\}$.
Now suppose that~$G'_i$ has a $2n$-colouring~$c'$.
As the $k$-type vertices form a clique, we may assume without loss of generality that $c'(k_\ell)=\ell$ for $\ell \in \{1,\ldots,2n\}$.
Hence the restriction of~$c'$ to~$G_i$ yields a colouring~$c$ that respects~$L$.\qed
\end{proof}

